# Code Style

## Code Style (Project Config)

### Current Project Practices (from analysis)

Match the style of existing code when generating new code:

- **Variable declarations**: use const/let
- **Function style**: arrow functions

### Run After Code Generation

\`\`\`bash
# Type check
npm run build
\`\`\`



## Do / Don't

\`\`\`typescript
// DON'T: use any — loses type safety
function process(data: any) { return data.value; }

// DO: use precise types
function process(data: ProcessInput): ProcessOutput {
  return data.value;
}
\`\`\`

\`\`\`typescript
// DON'T: implicit typing + mutable default
var count = 0;

// DO: explicit types + prefer immutability
const count: number = 0;
\`\`\`


## Project UI Stack

**Currently using**: antd component library

### Ant Design Conventions

**Do ✅**
\`\`\`tsx
// Prefer antd native APIs — do not re-wrap existing capabilities
import { Table, Form, Modal, Button, Space } from "antd";

// Use Form.useForm() — do not use a raw ref
const [form] = Form.useForm();

// Table pagination via onChange callback
<Table
  dataSource={data}
  columns={columns}
  pagination={{ current, pageSize, total, onChange: handlePageChange }}
/>;
\`\`\`

**Don't ❌**
\`\`\`tsx
// Do not use native <button> instead of antd Button
<button onClick={…}>Submit</button>

// Do not reimplement antd Modal.confirm / message.error
const MyAlert = () => <div className="alert">{msg}</div>;
\`\`\`

### Common Scenarios

| Scenario | Components |
|----------|------------|
| Data lists | \`Table\` |
| Form submission | \`Form\` + \`Form.useForm()\` |
| Confirmation dialogs | \`Modal.confirm()\` |
| Action feedback | \`message.success/error()\` |
| Loading states | \`Spin\` or Table \`loading\` prop |

### Accessibility (A11y) Minimum Requirements

- Interactive elements must have \`aria-label\` or visible text
- Icon buttons must include a \`title\` attribute
- Form fields must be associated with a \`label\` (htmlFor)


## Additional Best Practices

### Code Style:

Utilize TypeScript's type system fully: use interfaces, type aliases, and generics where appropriate

**Related Tech Stack**: TypeScript

---

### Exceptions

Use exceptions to handle errors you don't expect.

